\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction---prompting models with target schemas without fine-tuning---degrades sharply as schema complexity grows: NER recall drops from 0.72 to 0.42 with 64 candidate types, and RE accuracy falls from 85.7\% to 50.4\% with 80 relations. Prior work conflates type count with type confusability, leaving the mechanisms behind this degradation unclear. We conduct the first controlled empirical study that isolates type confusability from type count across three IE tasks (NER, RE, event detection) and three datasets. We introduce the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) to quantify pairwise semantic overlap between types and design two complementary experimental paradigms: count curves that vary schema size while randomizing confusability, and BCS-controlled experiments that fix size while varying confusability. Our experiments reveal two orthogonal degradation channels: a count channel that suppresses recall in NER and RE as candidate types increase, and a confusability channel where semantically similar types trigger intra-batch substitution errors. Pairwise SCS predicts type confusion across tasks (Pearson $r = 0.112$--$0.129$, $r^2 \approx 0.013$--$0.017$)---modest in magnitude but the first factor empirically isolated in controlled zero-shot IE experiments. Confusability effects amplify $3.4\times$ as schema size grows from 16 to 80 types, with accuracy gaps widening from 9.1 to 31.2 percentage points. We further identify a centrality confound that reverses per-type confusability aggregation on homogeneous schemas while the pairwise signal remains intact. Our confusability-aware decomposition (SDGD) yields an informative negative result: merge amplification destroys precision across all decomposition strategies, revealing a structural bottleneck for decompose-then-merge pipelines. These findings carry practical implications for schema design and identify merge amplification as the key obstacle for decomposition approaches in zero-shot IE.
\end{abstract}
